import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from con_helpers import *
save(4,6,"Living in Harmony: What Health Actually Feels Like","Choose one set of precious minutes, such as the first twenty after waking or the last twenty before sleep, protect it from the most agitating thing in the house, and put one settling thing in it. What do you notice?",[
mc("U","Kai says every wellness message he has seen sells a finish line. What does Swamiji say Ṛta is?",
 ["The natural baseline of a system in tune, approached, lost and returned to, with less effort to function.","A peak of experience, which a person reaches by great effort and then holds for as long as possible.","A state of freedom from all difficulty, in which nothing is ever struggled with or lost again.","A rule of conduct, set out by the old teachers for those who wish to live a good and orderly life."],0,
 "The four portraits are of ordinary people: a nurse who is tired some mornings, a teacher who still snaps, a studio owner who sometimes misses her window, a man and his mother. None is perfect. What they share is a direction: less noise in front, more signal coming through, the present moment becoming somewhere to be rather than escape.",
 "Ṛta is a baseline you keep returning to","Part 1"),
mc("A","Dr Watson says the nurse in her fifties did not optimise anything. What does Swamiji say she did?",
 ["Stopped fighting the rhythms that hold her and drew on them: evenings with the dark, moving, eating by hunger.","Followed a strict programme of diet and exercise laid down for her, which she keeps to even when tired.","Trained her heart directly by a daily routine, so that its good health is the result of that training.","Gave up her work on her feet, which allowed her to rest at all hours and sleep for as long as she liked."],0,
 "Her heart, measured, is unusually healthy for her age, and she trained none of it directly. It emerged from the shape of her days. This is health as alignment arising when the conditions are right, not as something held by force.",
 "She stopped overriding her body","Part 1"),
mc("A","At the end of a teaching day, James is spent but not hollow. What does the difference mean?",
 ["Spent means the energy was used; hollow means it was taken without return. Reading it shows your own coherence.","Spent means he has done too much and hollow means he has done too little, so he should aim between them.","Spent is a sign of failure and hollow is a sign of success, since a hollow day is one of quiet and ease.","Spent and hollow are two names for the same tiredness, which follows any day of hard work in a school."],0,
 "A mind in Ṛta knows this from the inside. On the spent-but-not-hollow days, look back and ask what gave the energy back. It is diagnostic and not prescriptive: learning to read your own coherence and to spot what restores it.",
 "Spent is energy used; hollow is energy taken","Part 1"),
mc("U","Five wind-up metronomes on a rocking board fall into unison with no conductor. Which idea does this show?",
 ["Entrainment: coupled rhythms find each other, as breath and heart do under your hand or two people in attention.","Resonance: a single note, matched to a glass, sets it ringing so that it draws on the surrounding air.","Conservation: the total energy of the five metronomes stays the same however they are set to tick.","Friction: the board slows the five of them till they come to rest at the same moment, so they agree."],0,
 "The tiny shared movement of the board lets each one feel the others. Between people, in choirs and meditation groups, heart rates and breathing drift toward each other: not sentiment, physics at the scale of a relationship.",
 "Entrainment: coupled rhythms synchronise","Part 2"),
judge("“A healthy heart beats at a flat, steady rate like a metronome, and a flat heartbeat is a sign of the best health.”",F,
 "A healthy heart speeds and slows constantly with the breath, and high heart-rate variability predicts better mood, immunity and resilience; a flat, metronomic heart is a warning sign. Breathing at about six breaths a minute locks heart, breath and blood-pressure reflex into one smooth wave.",
 "Health is living variability around a centre","Part 2"),
mc("U","Why is a coherent system efficient, according to Conversation 4.6?",
 ["Many rhythms lock into stable step, each held up by the others, so it costs far less energy to run.","One strong rhythm drowns out all the weaker ones, so the system has only one thing to do at a time.","Each part works on its own at full strength, so that the whole gets the most out of every part.","The rhythms slow down till they all run at the speed of the slowest, so that none of them tires."],0,
 "Not identical rhythms, but each knowing where the others are, like a whole board of metronomes swinging together. A body in coherence, heart, breath and brain in step, is at its most efficient. Tuned to the rhythms that hold you, you draw energy without effort; out of tune, you must generate everything yourself.",
 "Coherence is many rhythms held up by each other","Part 2"),
judge("“The Schumann resonance, the Earth’s faint hum, is firmly proved to entrain our brain rhythms and is a main support of the case.”",F,
 "The frequencies match intriguingly, but no mechanism has been shown and the studies conflict. Swamiji says to hold it as an open question and not a load-bearing one. The case stands on the heart, the brain and the body clock without it.",
 "Schumann resonance is an open question","Part 2"),
mc("A","Sarah realises she ‘relaxes’ with the most agitating thing in the house. What does the lesson say about this?",
 ["The mind is always tuning to something, so there is no neutral; choose the station for your first minutes.","Relaxing is a matter of taste, so what a person turns to at the end of a day does not make much difference.","The agitating thing is harmless, since only a strong shock, and not a steady hum, can leave any mark.","She should give up all screens for good, since each one is bound to pull her toward agitation."],0,
 "Entrainment is neutral: you fall into step with whatever holds you. Tune to something more coherent and you are drawn upward; to something more fragmented and you are dragged down. Guard one set of minutes, such as the first twenty after waking, and fill them with something settling.",
 "There is no neutral: choose what you tune to","Part 3"),
mc("A","Dr Watson has used breathwork, yoga, light therapy, mindfulness and social-rhythm therapy as separate techniques. How does Swamiji unify them?",
 ["Each restores one dimension of resonant fit, so ask which is most broken and re-tune that one first.","They compete, and the one with the best evidence should be used for every patient in the same way.","They are all the same technique under different names, so any one of them will serve in place of another.","They are for different diseases, so the diagnosis alone decides which of them a patient must be given."],0,
 "Breathwork restores heart-breath coherence, light therapy the body clock, mindfulness attention, yoga couples body and breath, and social-rhythm therapy the timing of meals and sleep. Choosing a practice is choosing which broken rhythm to re-tune first.",
 "Match the practice to the most disrupted dimension","Practical situation"),
mc("A","Kai wants proof, not trust. What two-part experiment does Swamiji set him?",
 ["Watch the metronomes or feel heart and breath under his hand, then audit a day of what he tunes to.","Read about entrainment in several books, then tell the group whether the writers agree with each other.","Ask others whether they have felt it, then go with whatever the larger number of them say they felt.","Leave the idea alone for a while, then return to it when he has slept better and can think about it."],0,
 "He makes the physics his own, then treats his own state as the readout: which things leave him more coherent and which leave him scattered. It is the sceptic’s way: find it out for yourself.",
 "Make the physics yours, then audit your stations","Practical situation")])
